# 22. Data that currently remains in memory

Some structures are declared in `store.ts` but are not part of the JSON persistence queue:

```text
reviews
fanPosts
fanConcepts
versionHistories
projectRelations
shareTokens
passwordResetTokens
```

That means their current behavior is different from users/projects/content.

For your architecture explanation, say:

> "PlotForge currently uses a hybrid in-memory + file-backed JSON persistence layer."

That is more accurate than simply saying "PlotForge has a database."

---

# 23. ID Generation

`store.ts` maintains counters such as:

```text
userIdCounter
projectIdCounter
commentIdCounter
invitationIdCounter
reviewIdCounter
fanPostIdCounter
fanConceptIdCounter
```

Then functions generate IDs:

```text
nextUserId()
nextProjectId()
nextCommentId()
...
```

This recently mattered because owner bootstrapping originally happened before the ID generator initialization. That Temporal Dead Zone problem was fixed by moving the generators above the bootstrap logic.

---

# 24. Owner Bootstrap

`store.ts` also creates the owner account from environment variables.

Relevant configuration:

```text
OWNER_EMAIL
OWNER_PASSWORD
OWNER_USERNAME
```

This prevents the platform owner from depending on a hardcoded privileged account inside the source code.

